title op3: the spurious rename/rename that no user performed

# The conflict the relativized reading deletes, and the only one of
# the ten where Section 16 changes a VERDICT rather than an edit list
# or a choice of dnode.  That makes it the sharpest.
#
# Onto renames the file:     /d/f becomes /d/g.
# Off-of renames the folder: /d becomes /e, and f rides along.
#
# Nobody renamed the same thing twice.  But under full-path naming
# pool 3 loses the name /d/f on BOTH sides, and gains /d/g on one
# side and /e/f on the other, so the one-move rule of Definition 9.3
# sees two disagreeing moves of one name and reports a conflict.  The
# user is then asked to resolve a collision between something they
# did to a file and something they did to its grandparent.
#
# Relativized: (base /d, onto /d) corresponds by green, and
# (base /d, off-of /e) by red.  Inside the second pair the leaf f is
# equal on both sides, so off-of changed no name below the directory
# at all.  Only onto renamed the child, one move decides, and the two
# changes compose.
#
# Hand-worked: clean, /e/g, every dnode preserved.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: d/
      3-100: f = fv0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: d/
      3-100: g = fv0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: e/
      3-100: f = fv0

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: e
      3-100: g = fv0
